---
import CommercialLayout from '../layouts/CommercialLayout.astro';
import BriefForm from '../components/services/BriefForm.astro';
---

<CommercialLayout
  title="Agency workflow partner"
  description="Bounded workflow delivery for agencies with explicit client authorization, access separation, attribution, review, and capacity."
>
  <header class="page-intro">
    <div class="commercial-shell page-intro__grid">
      <div>
        <h1 class="commercial-title">
          Your client relationship stays yours. The evidence stays honest.
        </h1><p>
          Agency partners can use the same Repair, Setup, and Care units with
          client permission, separate access, named reviewers, and a written
          delivery record. A consolidated relationship does not create an
          unlimited retainer.
        </p><div class="commercial-actions">
          <a
            class="commercial-action"
            href="/brief/?source=partner&context=Agency%20partner%20workflow&service=unsure"
            >Send an agency brief</a
          ><a
            class="commercial-action commercial-action--text"
            href="#partner-path">See the handoff</a
          >
        </div>
      </div><p class="page-intro__note">
        Initial workflow capacity<br /><strong
          >No more than 3 registered Care workflows globally</strong
        ><br />One active delivery project across direct and partner work.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="commercial-section commercial-section--ink" id="partner-path">
    <div class="commercial-shell">
      <div class="commercial-section__head">
        <h2>The agency handoff has four named owners.</h2><p
          class="commercial-section__intro"
        >
          The agreement decides what the end client sees, who authorizes access,
          and who approves customer-facing material.
        </p>
      </div><ol class="process-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Agency brief</strong><p>
            One workflow, expected result, systems, client context, and
            disclosure requirements.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Client authorization</strong><p>
            The actual account owner approves access and permitted test data
            before credentials move.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Acceptance review</strong><p>
            The agency or named client reviewer checks the agreed evidence in
            the nonproduction environment.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Delivery record</strong><p>
            Scope, authorship, test result, production handoff, and correction
            window remain traceable.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="commercial-section">
    <div class="commercial-shell">
      <div class="commercial-section__head">
        <h2>Boundaries that survive the deadline.</h2><p
          class="commercial-section__intro"
        >
          Partner delivery can be quiet without inventing people, credentials,
          or work history.
        </p>
      </div><div class="boundary-list">
        <div>
          <h3>Access separation</h3><p>
            Each client’s accounts, credentials, test records, and evidence stay
            scoped to that client. Shared agency access is used only when the
            client has authorized it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Attribution and disclosure</h3><p>
            The disclosure model follows the agreement. Commits and delivery
            history remain attributable to their actual author or an agreed
            system account.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Relationship protection</h3><p>
            The standard offered terms include confidentiality, defined
            deliverable assignment after final payment, and client
            non-solicitation during the engagement and for two years after the
            last invoice. The signed agreement controls the exact boundary.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Written communication</h3><p>
            Requests are consolidated in writing. Automated drafting may assist
            routine correspondence, but it is never represented as a human
            message when that distinction matters.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Review before delivery</h3><p>
            The agency can be the named reviewer for customer-facing material
            when the agreement says so. That review step becomes part of
            acceptance.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>No implied calls</h3><p>
            The workflow offer does not include client calls, standups, or live
            account management. If a separately scoped engagement needs named
            meeting participation, it is stated in writing.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="commercial-section commercial-section--ink">
    <div class="commercial-shell">
      <div class="commercial-section__head">
        <h2>The same published units apply.</h2><p
          class="commercial-section__intro"
        >
          Workflow Repair is $1,500, Setup is $3,000, and Care is $500 per
          registered workflow per month. Larger subcontracting, audits, and
          retainers preserve the existing partner rate card terms until
          separately revised in an agreement.
        </p>
      </div><div class="commercial-actions">
        <a class="commercial-action" href="/workflows/">Read workflow scope</a
        ><a class="commercial-action commercial-action--text" href="/services/"
          >See all service routes</a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="commercial-section" id="rate-card">
    <div class="commercial-shell">
      <div class="commercial-section__head">
        <h2>Existing partner rate card.</h2><p
          class="commercial-section__intro"
        >
          These larger subcontracting options remain available beside the fixed
          workflow units. The written proposal controls the actual scope.
        </p>
      </div><div class="offer-ledger">
        <div class="offer-row">
          <h3>White-label development</h3><p class="offer-price">$125/hr</p><p>
            Committed bulk engineering capacity after fit and access review.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="offer-row">
          <h3>Complex architecture</h3><p class="offer-price">$150–$175/hr</p><p
          >
            Auth, data, governed automation, and complex system boundaries.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="offer-row">
          <h3>Technical audit</h3><p class="offer-price">$2,500/project</p><p>
            Bounded pre-acquisition or pre-launch codebase review.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="offer-row">
          <h3>Emergency overflow</h3><p class="offer-price">$300/hr</p><p>
            Separately accepted deadline rescue with a four-hour minimum.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="commercial-section__head" style="margin-top: 3rem;">
        <h3>Partner retainers</h3><p class="commercial-section__intro">
          Partner Lite remains $2,500/month for 20 hours, Partner Standard
          remains $5,000/month for 40 hours, and Partner Plus remains
          $10,000/month for 80 hours. These are capacity retainers, not open
          workflow monitoring or unlimited work.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="commercial-shell">
    <BriefForm
      source="partner"
      context="Agency partner workflow inquiry"
      heading="Send the agency brief"
    />
  </div>
</CommercialLayout>
